# Accessibility

- Start with semantic HTML and native controls; add ARIA only when native semantics cannot express the behavior.
- Give controls stable accessible names and useful state or error descriptions.
- Support keyboard, pointer, and touch interaction without nested interactive elements.
- Keep focus visible, move it intentionally for dialogs and route-level errors, and restore it when transient UI closes.
- Preserve heading order, landmarks, label-control relationships, and meaningful alternative text.
- Allow text to resize without clipping; avoid fixed heights for text-bearing controls.
- Respect reduced-motion and forced-color preferences, and do not use color alone to communicate state.
- Use live regions sparingly and announce only meaningful asynchronous changes.
- Test tab order, activation keys, zoom or larger default text, and at least one accessibility tree inspection.
